BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:upe-foundation-curatorial-fellowship-camden-art-centre-2026@artificialn
 ouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: Upe Foundation Curatorial Fellowship at Camden Art Centre
  2026
DESCRIPTION:Upe Foundation is launching the Upe Foundation Curatorial Fello
 wship at Camden Art Centre as part of its Fall 2026 Curatorial Fellowship 
 Programme. The opportunity invites Baltic early-career curators to apply f
 or a full-time curatorial role at Camden Art Centre\, an institution inter
 nationally acclaimed for its rigorous exhibitions\, residencies\, events a
 nd learning programmes. The fellowship is a full-time\, 12-month position 
 starting Autumn 2026\, with the possibility of a further 6-month extension
  (18 months total) upon review and mutual decision\; relocation to London 
 is required. ROLE: embedded within Camden Art Centre's Exhibitions Departm
 ent\, the Fellow works as Assistant Curator\, supporting the development\,
  coordination and delivery of the exhibitions programme and related projec
 ts and events\, with practical experience across exhibition research\, pro
 duction\, interpretation\, publications\, public programmes\, loans\, inst
 allation and documentation\, and liaising with artists\, galleries\, lende
 rs\, partner organisations\, writers and designers. The Fellow also shares
  research connected to Baltic and broader Central Eastern European artisti
 c contexts. ELIGIBILITY: a demonstrated connection to or sustained engagem
 ent with the Baltic region (Lithuania\, Latvia\, Estonia\, or their diaspo
 ras)\, which may include lived experience\, heritage\, working knowledge o
 f a Baltic language\, relevant research\, or curatorial work with Baltic a
 rtists/institutions/contexts\; early-career curatorial experience (freelan
 ce\, self-organised\, project-based or institutional)\; and a higher educa
 tion qualification (BA\, MA or equivalent) in curating\, art history\, vis
 ual culture or related disciplines. Desirable: working knowledge of Lithua
 nian\, Latvian or Estonian\; fluency in a second language\; experience wit
 h Articheck and with CRM systems (particularly Salesforce). Applicants mus
 t hold the right to work in the UK (via the under-26 'new entrant' Skilled
  Worker route\, the Creative Worker visa route\, or existing right to work
  such as British citizenship or settled/pre-settled status). OFFER: GBP 30
 \,960 salary\, structured professional development within the Exhibitions 
 team\, and opportunities to build professional networks in London and inte
 rnationally. Applications reviewed jointly by Upe Foundation and Camden Ar
 t Centre\, with first-round online interviews and a second interview for f
 inalists. The organizers strongly encourage applicants to write materials 
 in their own words and avoid generic or AI-generated responses. TIMELINE: 
 application deadline 12:00 noon UK time\, Monday 13 July 2026\; interviews
  6 August 2026\; final decision mid-August 2026\; start Autumn 2026. Quest
 ions: recruitment@camdenartcentre.org.\n\nOrganisation: Upe Foundation (in
  partnership with Camden Art Centre)\nLocation: London\, United Kingdom. F
 ull-time\, in-person\; relocation to London is required. Applicants must h
 old the right to work in the UK.\nAward: Salary of GBP 30\,960 per year. A
  full-time Assistant Curator role on a 12-month fixed-term contract\, with
  the possibility of a further 6-month extension (18 months total) subject 
 to formal review and mutual agreement.\nDuration: 12-month fixed-term posi
 tion starting Autumn 2026\, with possible 6-month extension (18 months tot
 al).\n\nMore: https://upefoundation.org/news/open-call-upe-foundation-cura
 torial-fellowship-at-camden-art-centre?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://upefoundation.org/news/open-call-upe-foundation-curatorial-fell
 owship-at-camden-art-centre?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:London\, United Kingdom. Full-time\, in-person\; relocation to Lon
 don is required. Applicants must hold the right to work in the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:british-council-iash-90th-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: British Council 90th Anniversary Research Fellowships at 
 IASH 2027 (Edinburgh)
DESCRIPTION:Twelve-month postdoctoral fellowship from the Institute for Adv
 anced Studies in the Humanities (IASH) at the University of Edinburgh\, in
  partnership with the British Council\, marking the Council's 90th anniver
 sary. Up to two fellowships per year as part of the 2025-2027 partnership.
  Fellows spend ten months residential at IASH (January to October 2027) fo
 llowed by up to two months of knowledge-exchange and dissemination work in
  their home country in collaboration with the British Council. ELIGIBILITY
 : postdoctoral researchers based in an ODA-recipient country where the Bri
 tish Council operates (full list of ~60+ countries published on the IASH p
 age\, spanning Albania to Zimbabwe and including the Occupied Palestinian 
 Territories)\; PhD completed within the last seven years (career breaks ex
 cluded from the seven-year window)\; applicants must not hold a permanent 
 university position and must not have held a prior IASH Fellowship. Resear
 ch themes should align with British Council priorities across Arts\, Educa
 tion and English language\, plus cross-cutting interests in international 
 relations\, soft power\, international development\, peace building\, and 
 cultural relations and diplomacy. NOTE: applicants are required to contact
  relevant University of Edinburgh researchers before submitting\; informat
 ional webinar 26 May 2026. Decisions communicated late September 2026. Ref
 erences (minimum two\, maximum three) must be emailed by referees directly
  to iash@ed.ac.uk by the deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Institute for Advanced
  Studies in the Humanities (IASH)\, University of Edinburgh\, in partnersh
 ip with the British Council\nLocation: Edinburgh\, UK (10 months residenti
 al at IASH\; final 2 months in home country)\nAward: GBP 2\,500 per month 
 bursary for 12 months\, plus travel expenses\, dedicated office space\, Un
 iversity of Edinburgh email and library access\, a University mentor\, wee
 kly Fellows' Lunch\, work-in-progress seminars\, full calendar of Institut
 e and College events\, and opportunities to participate in or design funde
 d workshops and colloquia. No application fee.\nDuration: 12 months (10 mo
 nths residential at IASH\, January to October 2027\; followed by November-
 December 2027 knowledge-exchange in the home country)\n\nMore: https://www
 .iash.ed.ac.uk/british-council-90th-anniversary-research-fellowships?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iash.ed.ac.uk/british-council-90th-anniversary-research-fel
 lowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Edinburgh\, UK (10 months residential at IASH\; final 2 months in 
 home country)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-uk-winter-fellowship-2027-research-track@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI UK Winter Fellowship 2027 - Research Track
DESCRIPTION:GovAI's UK Winter Fellowship (Research Track) is a three-month 
 in-person fellowship in London for researchers and policy professionals se
 eking to enter or advance careers in AI governance and policy. Fellows con
 duct independent research on a topic of their choice\, paired with a GovAI
  supervisor from the team or network\; the first two weeks are spent explo
 ring the AI governance landscape before a research proposal is finalised. 
 Research outputs may include a report\, white paper\, journal article\, op
 -ed\, or blog post targeted at an audience relevant to AI governance. Alon
 gside the project\, fellows attend expert Q&As\, weekly seminars\, skills 
 workshops\, work-in-progress meetings\, and social events\, and are encour
 aged to discuss follow-on career opportunities with GovAI's team and netwo
 rk. GovAI alumni have gone on to roles in US\, UK\, and EU government\; le
 ading AI companies (Anthropic\, DeepMind\, OpenAI)\; top think tanks (CSET
 \, RAND)\; and leading universities (Oxford\, Cambridge). AMOUNT: £12\,00
 0 salary for three months\, plus travel support to London and weekday lunc
 h. Visa sponsorship for a 3-month temporary work visa is available\; succe
 ssful visa applicants must remain in their country of application while th
 e visa is processed. ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of backgrounds (gov
 ernment\, academia\, industry\, civil society)\; no strict degree requirem
 ents\; relevant areas include public policy\, political science\, computer
  science\, economics\, biosecurity\, cybersecurity\, China studies\, and r
 isk management. Senior candidates who need accommodations should email fel
 lowships@governance.ai with subject line 'senior candidate winter fellowsh
 ip 2027' and a CV. SELECTION CRITERIA: quality and clarity of written work
 \, relevant expertise\, judgement in prioritising research directions\, an
 d team fit (intellectual honesty\, openness to feedback\, comfort with unc
 ertainty). APPLICATION PROCESS: (1) written application and automated abst
 ract reasoning assessment\; (2) paid remote work test (August 2026)\; (3) 
 remote interview and reference checks (September 2026)\; decisions communi
 cated October 2026. Deadline: 12 July 2026\, 23:59 BST.\n\nOrganisation: C
 entre for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK\; in-person\
 , full-time.\nAward: £12\,000 salary for the three-month term\, plus trav
 el support to London and weekday lunch provided. Visa sponsorship (3-month
  temporary work visa) available. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 months\,
  18 January to 9 April 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/post/winte
 r-fellowship-2027-research-track?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/post/winter-fellowship-2027-research-track?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK\; in-person\, full-time.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ual-storytelling-research-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: UAL AKO Storytelling Institute: Storytelling Research Fel
 lows 2026 (six posts)
DESCRIPTION:The UAL AKO Storytelling Institute\, with the Power of Pop Fund
 \, is recruiting six Storytelling Research Fellows to run independent rese
 arch projects on how storytelling in popular culture shapes attitudes\, be
 haviour and public discourse. Fellows work across media including televisi
 on\, film\, gaming\, podcasts and digital culture\, are supported by the I
 nstitute's Head of Research and a peer cohort\, and produce actionable fin
 dings for creative practitioners\, funders and social-impact organisations
 \, presented at a closing public symposium. ELIGIBILITY: experienced resea
 rchers with a PhD or equivalent research expertise gained academically\, i
 ndependently or through professional practice\, with a track record of del
 ivering high-quality research and working with external partners. Practice
 -based and non-academic researchers are explicitly in scope. APPLICATION: 
 CV\, record of publications\, a research proposal of 1\,000 to 1\,200 word
 s\, and a supporting statement including at least one example of a researc
 h project you designed and delivered. Applications via the UAL jobs portal
 \; closes 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of the Arts Londo
 n (UAL AKO Storytelling Institute)\, with the Power of Pop Fund\nLocation:
  High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)\nAward: GBP 48\,563 per a
 nnum pro rata (around GBP 19\,425 for the part-time fellowship hours)\, pl
 us UAL benefits. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine-month fellowship\, pa
 rt-time\, culminating in a public symposium\n\nMore: https://jobs.arts.ac.
 uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobs.arts.ac.uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bold-lab-bold-fellow-oxford-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: BOLD Fellow\, British Open-ended Learning and Discovery L
 ab
DESCRIPTION:The British Open-ended Learning and Discovery (BOLD) Lab is a n
 ew UKRI-funded initiative establishing an AI research laboratory led from 
 the University of Oxford\, bringing more than 80 researchers from Oxford\,
  UCL and Imperial College London under one vision. It carries an initial i
 nvestment of around GBP 8 million with the potential for a further GBP 22 
 million extending the investment over six years\, making it one of the UK'
 s largest bets on fundamental AI research. The lab's remit is ambitious\, 
 high-risk work on the fundamental science of artificial intelligence\, alo
 ngside training future leaders in the field. THE ROLE: up to 12 BOLD Fello
 ws are being recruited across the three universities. Fellows are expected
  to operate with a high level of independence and responsibility\, setting
  their own research direction\, publishing at the highest level\, and ment
 oring junior researchers and doctoral students. They also play a leading r
 ole in managing the lab and take responsibility for developing its strateg
 ic and scientific direction\, with primary responsibility for a programme 
 of independent funded research projects. The posting frames the draw as ex
 ceptional independence\, direct collaboration with world-leading colleague
 s across the three institutions\, access to substantial and rapidly growin
 g compute\, and real influence over the intellectual direction and culture
  of a major national lab. WHO SHOULD APPLY: senior researchers with an est
 ablished\, original research track record in AI or a closely related field
 \, who are a recognised authority in their area. HOW TO APPLY: submit an o
 nline application with a covering letter or supporting statement\, a CV\, 
 and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed beforehand to
  the BOLD Exec Team at boldexec-team@eng.ox.ac.uk. NOTE: this is a salarie
 d fixed-term research post rather than a grant. Only applications received
  before midday on Tuesday 15 September 2026 can be considered.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: University of Oxford\, Department of Engineering Science (BOLD Lab\,
  with UCL and Imperial College London)\nLocation: Oxford\, UK\, with posts
  also based at UCL and Imperial College London. The 12 posts are employed 
 across the three universities.\nAward: Salary on Oxford Grade 10R\, GBP 65
 \,336 to GBP 84\,663 per year\, with the posting noting that further salar
 y options are being explored. Benefits include associate membership at Reu
 ben College\, 38 days annual leave\, pension scheme\, childcare services\,
  and travel expenses for stakeholder and conference visits. Fellows also g
 et access to substantial compute: 3 million GPU hours on Isambard for the 
 first 18 months\, with the lab working towards 5\,000 H100-equivalents in 
 total across industry and government support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Fixed-term\, 12 months\, funded by UKRI\n\nMore: https://eng.ox.ac.uk/j
 obs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Oxford\, UK\, with posts also based at UCL and Imperial College Lo
 ndon. The 12 posts are employed across the three universities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:era-ai-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: ERA:AI Fellowship 2027 (Winter cohort\, Cambridge)
DESCRIPTION:Paid in-person research fellowship in Cambridge for people who 
 want to build expertise in AI safety or AI governance. Fellows develop and
  complete a research project on mitigating risks from frontier AI\, workin
 g in one of three streams: technical safety\, governance\, or technical AI
  governance. WHAT YOU GET: a GBP 10\,000 stipend for the 10-week programme
 \, visa support\, travel expense coverage\, meals during working hours\, e
 xpert mentorship from established researchers\, dedicated research managem
 ent support aimed at publishing in top venues\, and immersion in Cambridge
 's AI safety research community with regular social and networking events.
  Top performers can be extended by 6 months or more through the Research S
 cholars programme. ELIGIBILITY: open to anyone 18 or older\; the programme
  is talent-first with no formal credential requirement\, welcoming researc
 hers\, students and professionals from technical\, policy\, security\, leg
 al\, economic and other relevant backgrounds\; prior formal research exper
 ience is not required. SELECTION: three stages\, a written application of 
 roughly 2 hours\, Round 1 interviews in late September for the top 20 perc
 ent\, and Round 2 interviews in early October. COHORT: starts 18 January 2
 027. Deadline 13 September 2026 at 23:59 Anywhere on Earth.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: ERA Fellowship\nLocation: Cambridge\, United Kingdom\, in person with d
 edicated desk space at ERA's office. Visa support provided.\nAward: GBP 10
 \,000 stipend for the 10-week fellowship\, plus visa support\, travel expe
 nse coverage and meals during working hours. Top performers can receive 6+
  month extensions through the Research Scholars programme. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: 10 weeks\, 18 January to late March 2027. Deadline 13 Sept
 ember 2026 at 23:59 Anywhere on Earth.\n\nMore: https://erafellowship.org/
 fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://erafellowship.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cambridge\, United Kingdom\, in person with dedicated desk space a
 t ERA's office. Visa support provided.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lasr-labs-winter-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260920
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260921
SUMMARY:Deadline: LASR Labs Winter 2027 (London AI Safety Research)
DESCRIPTION:LASR Labs is a 13-week full-time technical AI safety research p
 rogramme in London: teams of 3 to 4 write a technical paper on loss-of-con
 trol risks under an experienced supervisor\, with recent cohorts publishin
 g at NeurIPS\, ICLR and ICML workshops\, and around 90 percent of alumni m
 oving into AI safety or security roles. FUNDING: GBP 15\,000 stipend plus 
 food\, office space\, and flights/travel. ELIGIBILITY: technical machine-l
 earning or quantitative background\, from undergraduates to experienced en
 gineers\, with no hard requirements\; international applicants get visa su
 pport via the UK Government Authorised Exchange programme. Hosted at the L
 ondon Initiative for Safe AI. Runs 11 January to 9 April 2027. DEADLINE: 2
 0 September 2026\, 23:59 GMT.\n\nOrganisation: LASR Labs\, hosted at the L
 ondon Initiative for Safe AI (LISA)\nLocation: London\, UK (in-person\, fu
 ll-time). International applicants eligible\; visa support via the UK Auth
 orised Exchange programme for those without UK work rights.\nAward: GBP 15
 \,000 stipend for the 13-week programme\, plus food\, office space\, and f
 lights/travel. No application fee.\nDuration: 13 weeks\, 11 January to 9 A
 pril 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.lasrlabs.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.lasrlabs.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (in-person\, full-time). International applicants elig
 ible\; visa support via the UK Authorised Exchange programme for those wit
 hout UK work rights.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iliad-fellowship-fall-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: ILIAD Fellowship: Mentored AI Alignment Research (Novembe
 r and December 2026 cohorts)
DESCRIPTION:The ILIAD Fellowship is a 3-month mentored research fellowship 
 producing concrete outputs in applied-mathematical AI alignment research\,
  distinct from ILIAD's four-week taught Intensive. Fellows work in person 
 in London or the SF Bay Area with a USD 6\,000 per month travel-and-housin
 g allowance. ELIGIBILITY: primarily PhD and postdoc researchers in mathema
 tics\, theoretical physics\, theoretical computer science or related field
 s\, though a degree is not a hard requirement given substantial research e
 xperience\; limited remote flexibility. DEADLINES: 21 September 2026 for t
 he November cohort (2 November 2026 to 5 February 2027)\; 19 October 2026 
 for the December cohort (1 December 2026 to 5 March 2027). A single common
  application at iliad.ac/apply covers the Fellowship and the Intensive.\n\
 nOrganisation: ILIAD\nLocation: London\, UK or San Francisco Bay Area\, US
 A (in-person\, limited remote flexibility). Primarily for PhD/postdoc-leve
 l researchers in maths\, theoretical physics\, or theoretical CS\; a degre
 e is not a hard requirement given substantial research experience.\nAward:
  USD 6\,000 per month travel-and-housing allowance for the 3-month fellows
 hip. No tuition or application fee.\nDuration: 3 months. November cohort: 
 2 November 2026 to 5 February 2027\; December cohort: 1 December 2026 to 5
  March 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.iliad.ac/fellowship?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iliad.ac/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK or San Francisco Bay Area\, USA (in-person\, limited r
 emote flexibility). Primarily for PhD/postdoc-level researchers in maths\,
  theoretical physics\, or theoretical CS\; a degree is not a hard requirem
 ent given substantial research experience.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260903T142650Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields. COMPENSATION: USD 155\,000 t
 o 285\,000 per year gross depending on location\, seniority and experience
 \, plus insurance\, retirement plan\, PTO (17 days US / 20 days UK)\, conf
 erence funding\, catered meals\, and up to USD 25\,000 in relocation suppo
 rt. ELIGIBILITY: at least one substantial piece of research-caliber work\;
  credentials are not required. Full-time (part-time case by case for acade
 mics on sabbatical)\, in person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or 
 Washington DC\, with visa support where required. TIMELINE: the Winter 202
 7 intake window runs 25 August to 31 October 2026 (AoE)\, with decisions b
 y end of November and interviews plus a paid work test in the first week o
 f December\; three intake windows per year.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Alig
 nment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berkele
 y\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. For 
 experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-ca
 liber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by locati
 on\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insuran
 ce\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meals\
 , and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuration:
  6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year.\
 n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
